                        "glosses": [
                            "The source of water for a stream, spring or well; a fountainhead; a wellhead."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": []
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "a1f13d92-addd-3c20-9504-2106cedee2af",
                        "entry_id": "1cc0960e-961d-3e69-b403-df511ca5bae8",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A perennial source of anything; a fountainhead of supply or emanation; resource."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "figuratively"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "f0e19a41-a56e-4b79-ab5f-8cf2b3bec6fb",
                                "sense_id": "a1f13d92-addd-3c20-9504-2106cedee2af",
                                "text": "We’re so often taught to simply believe in ourselves—to trust our instincts—that to see a narrative in which second-guessing oneself and refusing one’s deepest impulses is a potential wellspring of charity and good is, in itself, noteworthy."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "22a6e62b-85cd-4238-988e-0b20229bd210",
                                "sense_id": "a1f13d92-addd-3c20-9504-2106cedee2af",
                                "text": "Krapp’s Last Tape has been acted by prisoners in San Quentin and made into a chamber opera. It goes to the wellspring of an actor. Well, most of the time: when Albert Finney played the part, Beckett went to sleep in rehearsals."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
